cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you cite this work, please use the metadata below."
title: "FlyBrain: teaching a 27-cell mushroom-body model to read, and proving what it cannot read"
version: 0.2.0
abstract: >-
  A spiking classifier whose only plastic synapses are the KC->MBON weights of a
  mushroom-body model, trained on a hand-drawn 5x7 bitmap alphabet with
  three-factor reward-modulated STDP. The circuit reaches 100.0% holdout
  accuracy on 27 symbols (26 letters plus a blank) and is then shown ten digits
  it has no output cell for. It answers all 300 digit presentations with a
  letter, gets 0 correct, and its weight matrices and checkpoint are
  byte-identical before and after - the negative control is hashed rather than
  asserted. From v0.2.0 the fixed PN->KC expansion can be loaded from the
  measured hemibrain connectome instead of a random generator, so the same
  experiment runs on real detected wiring; a connectome replaces the wiring, not
  the task, and the 5x7 glyph encoding, the 27 output labels and the mapping of
  68 real MBONs onto 27 letters all remain inventions of this project.
authors:
  - name: "AscendedStarr"
type: software
license: MIT
date-released: "2026-08-31"
keywords:
  - spiking neural network
  - mushroom body
  - reward-modulated STDP
  - three-factor learning
  - optical character recognition
  - negative control
  - reproducibility
  - connectome
  - Drosophila melanogaster
  - hemibrain

repository-code: "https://github.com/AscendedStarr/FruitFlyBrain_AlphabetTrainings"

